It started in the late 1990s in St. Joseph, Missouri, a quiet Midwestern town where nothing about Mike Marcum suggested he would become an internet mystery. He wasn’t a physicist, didn’t have a lab, and had no academic background in advanced science. What he did have was an obsession with Nikola Tesla, free energy, and time travel.

Mike believed modern physics was lying to the public. He was convinced that corporations and governments had buried Tesla’s real discoveries, especially the ability to manipulate time using electromagnetic fields. Friends later said Mike talked constantly about “frequency,” “resonance,” and “hidden equations,” even though his explanations were often inconsistent and vague.

Around 1999, Mike began building a machine inside a rented storage unit. Witnesses described it as a chair or frame surrounded by coils, wires, copper tubing, and car batteries. He claimed it could bend time by creating a localized electromagnetic distortion.

Mike told friends he had already tested it.
According to him, during an early experiment, he jumped forward in time by several hours. He said the sky looked different, and when he returned, his watch was wrong. To others, this sounded like delusion. To Mike, it was proof.

In 2000, Mike attempted a full-scale test. He allegedly illegally tapped into the local power grid, pulling massive amounts of electricity into his storage unit. A neighborhood-wide blackout.
Police and utility workers arrived quickly. Inside the unit, they found scorched equipment and signs of an overloaded system. Mike was arrested for stealing electricity and briefly jailed.

After his release, Mike became more secretive. He told a small circle of friends that he was preparing one last test, this time with improvements that would prevent another blackout.

Then, in 2003, Mike Marcum vanished.
No goodbye. No machine. No body. Just vanished.

His storage unit was empty. His belongings were gone. His family said he never contacted them again. There was no financial activity, no confirmed sightings, and no official explanation.
